course: dictionary
lesson: 5961
title: Math and maths: one word, two spellings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Math is a noun. It is the name of a school subject about numbers, shapes, measuring, patterns and data. People in the USA and Canada write math. People in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand write maths. Both spellings mean the same thing.
explain: Math can also mean working with numbers. «Do the math» means work it out. Look at the words around it to see which meaning is being used. «Ollie learnt his seven and eight times tables from a maths game and got them all right in the test» tells you maths is the school subject, because it mentions a test and times tables.
explain: Math is useful outside school too: money, cooking, time, building and science all use it. «Some answers came first from pure reasoning: Neptune was found in 1846 exactly where maths said a hidden planet must be pulling Uranus.» Here maths means careful working with numbers and logic.
fact: Math is a noun.
fact: Math means the school subject about numbers, shapes, measuring, patterns and data.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, people write maths. Both spellings mean the same thing.
fact: «Do the math» means work it out.
fact: Math helps with money, cooking, time, building and science.
exercise: «In maths Josh stares at the clock.» What is maths here? | A kind of clock | A game of cards | A type of lunch | *The school subject
exercise: Dad says, «Do the math before you spend that money.» What does do the math mean? | Clean the kitchen | *Work out the numbers | Buy a new game | Sing a song
exercise: A child in London writes about the school subject. Which spelling would she use? | *Maths | Mth | Math | Mathe
test: In «Do the math», what does math mean? | A long walk | A hidden planet | A kind of fish | *Working with numbers, working it out
test: Which word is the name of a school subject about numbers and shapes? | Assembly | Lunch | *Math | Recess
exam: Which statement about math and maths is true? | Math only means shapes | Maths is a planet | *Both words mean the same school subject about numbers | They mean opposite things
